<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <!-- LatestMajor, not LatestMinor: type providers erase to the analyzed
         project's RUNTIME references, and a net8 host handed lib/net10.0
         SQLProvider assemblies cannot instantiate them ("SqlDataProvider is
         not defined" on a project that builds cleanly). Running on the
         newest installed runtime lets the tool typecheck projects that
         target it; machines with only net8 still run on net8. -->
    <RollForward>LatestMajor</RollForward>
    <!-- ships as a dotnet tool: dotnet tool install fsharp-refactor -->
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>fsharp-refactor</ToolCommandName>
    <PackageId>fsharp-refactor</PackageId>
    <Description>Applies the FSharp.Refactor analyzers' quick fixes to F# code from the command line. Point it at a project, a script, a solution, a directory or a glob; --dry-run reports without editing. The tool clarifies the (agent-generated or old) code's intent, reduces non-alphanumeric noise, and uses built-in .NET libraries more efficiently.</Description>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>0.8.32: FR0173 takes a mapper spanning lines only as a lambda whose body starts on a line of its own. A `match` or a first statement after the arrow moves with that line: `(fun i -&gt; match i with` over arms aligned to the `match` fell offside (FS0058) when the count&apos;s `max 0` made the line longer, and a second statement aligned to a first one became its argument when the line got shorter - `tap i` over `i` read as `tap i i`, a changed result with no compile error to stop it.

 0.8.31: FR0035 converts a list whose element can carry a float to a `Set` only when every element is a written-out non-NaN constant, since the Set finds a NaN `List.contains` never did. FR0167&apos;s String twins go through `nonNull` on FSharp.Core 9+, which throws on a null string as the copy did, so a sweep applies them. FR0041 sweeps `Array.contains v arr` on `int[]`/`int64[]` to `Enumerable.Contains(arr, v)`, exact on those types; the aggregations stay notes. FR0174 sweeps a nullable column of an exact type compared by `=`/`&lt;`/`&lt;=`/`&gt;`/`&gt;=` with a non-null value outside any `not`, where SQL and .NET both drop the NULL row. FR0071 no longer hoists a division by a signed integral `-1` (`MinValue / -1` overflows) or anything in a file under `open Checked`.

 0.8.30: FR0174 (performance, fix), CR0178's twin: in C#-style LINQ over `open System.Linq`, a query copied before `Where`/`Select` loads every row - `db.Orders.ToList().Where(fun o -&gt; o.Total &gt; 0).Select(fun o -&gt; o.Id)` becomes `db.Orders.Where(fun o -&gt; o.Total &gt; 0).Select(fun o -&gt; o.Id).ToList()`, for value-column comparisons and projections only, and by a sweep only where the chain's new List type binds as the IEnumerable did; the pipeline shape is behind the default-off `pipelines` knob. A review of the whole code closed some eighty defects, each reproduced by a failing test: non-UTF-8 files were rewritten with U+FFFD (now read in the system code page and put back byte for byte), the verification build stopped at its first failure without building consumers, a timeout left earlier passes' fixes unverified, and silent rewrites in FR0166, FR0010, FR0035, FR0021, FR0006, FR0170, FR0060, FR0075, FR0004, FR0005, FR0119, FR0049, FR0118, FR0160, FR0071, FR0026, FR0053, FR0023, FR0029, FR0034 and FR0157 (record-field rewrites now editor-only) got guards; FR0049 reads `winner = work &amp;&amp; work.IsCompleted &amp;&amp; work.Result` as a read; a C# consumer's escalated analyzer error (CA/IDE) counts as a code error.

 0.8.29: FR0173 (performance, fix): a range built only to be mapped over allocates a second collection the size of the result - `[| 0 .. dimension - 1 |] |&gt; Array.map f` is `Array.init dimension f`. Measured (.NET 10, a million elements): 7.0 ms and 8,000,099 B become 3.3 ms and 4,000,044 B, twice as fast on half the allocation. A sweep applies it only where the count is provably non-negative (a length, a count, a literal), since the empty range maps to an empty result where init raises ArgumentException; the editor offers it either way. FR0172 (idiom, fix): a match arm that binds a whole list only to read `itms.[0]` (or `itms[0]`, `itms.Head`, `List.head itms`), `itms.[1]` or `itms.Tail` is a cons pattern - `| itms -> g itms.[0]` becomes `| itmsHead :: _ -> g itmsHead`, the tail named only where the arm reads it. Exact where an earlier unguarded `[]` arm (and `[_]` for a second element) already keeps the short lists out of the arm, so the match stays as complete as it was and the compiler's exhaustiveness check stands in for the ArgumentException a list index raises; any other use of the name, an index above 1, a rebinding inside the arm, or an array (`[||]`) or an earlier bare catch-all (which covers every array and string too) stands the rule down. Parse-only, the `[]` arm settling the type. Measured: no run-time difference between the shapes - `xs.[0]` walks no cells - so the rule is the idiom, not speed. FR0156 takes the `let mutable` list beside the ResizeArray: `let mutable xs = []` fed by `xs &lt;- xs @ [ e ]` or `List.append xs [ e ]` inside its loops and only read after becomes the list expression with each element as its yield (measured at 1000 elements: 250x faster on 0.3% of the allocation - every append copied the list), and `xs &lt;- e :: xs` read through `List.rev` alone does too, the reverse becoming the bare name (1.5x on half); an assignment after the loops, a read inside them, a list fed at both ends or a project's own `@` stands it down. FR0050 leaves that combine alone (a fold would keep the copy), and FR0051's note names the list expression first. PerfClaims gains the cases FR0168, FR0170 and FR0171 had not got; FR0171's runs long enough to tier up, since a byte string literal is `InitializeArray` from RVA data, a helper call at tier 0 and a copy at tier 1. FR0071 now leaves an array literal inside the loop where it still hoists a list: a list is immutable, so n allocations become one and nothing can tell, but an array literal is a fresh mutable buffer per iteration and a hoisted one is shared by every pass. FR0015 per-call hoist knob: { "FR0015": { "perCall": false } } keeps the loop hoists and leaves a Regex in a plain function body where it is written; on by default, since a function is far likelier to be called many times than a module is to be initialised with its hoisted binding never read.

 0.8.28: A guard and rule exchange with CSharp.Refactor, twin by twin. Guards: FR0004 proves the source pure before a consumer that stops early (`exists`, `find`, `head`) - `Seq.exists` over a `seq { }` with an effect would skip some of it; FR0016 and FR0070 refuse a struct the file boxes, locks or null-tests, one implementing an interface, or one over 32 bytes inline (four decimals copy slower than they allocate); FR0075 and FR0032 pass over `HttpClient`, `HttpRequestMessage` and its contents, `SemaphoreSlim`, `ManualResetEventSlim` and `Task` (no resource the scope owns, and a handler mock reads the request back after the send); FR0151 knows `AggregateException` (InnerExceptions), `SqlException` (Number) and `FileNotFoundException` (FileName); FR0037's HttpClient note and FR0165 stand down in test files; FR0142 accepts TUnit. FR0015 hoists a Regex from any function body, not only a loop - a function is called from loops the file cannot see, and a construction is parsed per call. Fixes: FR0118's unobserved loop gains `ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` as its first statement; FR0164's filter shape becomes the list's own `RemoveAll`. Four rules: FR0168 (performance, fix) `try T.Parse s with _ -> 0` is `match T.TryParse s with`, a sweep applying it where the catch covers everything TryParse answers false to, FR0055 standing down there; FR0169 (correctness, note) a `seq` parameter or local enumerated twice on one path; FR0170 (performance, fix) `for k in d.Keys do ... d.[k]` is `for KeyValue(k, v) in d do`; FR0171 (performance, fix) `Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes "OK"` is `"OK"B`.

 0.8.27: Seven correctness rules, the F# twins of CSharp.Refactor's CR0160-CR0171 family where F# does not rule the hazard out by design. FR0159: `float (sum / count)` divides as integers before it widens - the editor offers `float sum / float count`, a sweep never applies it, and a literal operand or a product dividend (`float (screenW / 2)`, `float (x * mw / tw)`: a pixel centre, a scaled coordinate) is the editor's alone unless `{ "FR0159": { "all": true } }`. FR0160: a handler raising a new exception without the one it caught gains it as the constructor's trailing argument where the type has that constructor (`raise (X("m", ex))`, a `_`/`:? T` pattern gaining `as ex`); `failwith` in a handler that never reads what it caught is a note. FR0161: a mutating method on the struct a property returns mutates a copy - the one such copy F# does not refuse at compile time. FR0162: check-then-assign on a module-level or `static let` mutable races; `lazy` runs the factory once. FR0163: a `System.Threading.Timer` nothing references is collected mid-flight (a `System.Timers.Timer` is not - measured). FR0164: a collection edited inside a `for` over itself throws at the next step; the loop walks `Array.ofSeq items`. FR0165: a local `DateTime` compared with or subtracted from a UTC one flips with the timezone. Three rules widen: FR0055 notes the narrow catch that is TryParse as control flow (`with :? FormatException -&gt; 0`) and offers the rewrite where the catch covers what TryParse turns into false; FR0118 notes an awaiting loop under a token that never observes it; FR0123 notes `SemaphoreSlim.Wait`/`ReaderWriterLockSlim.Enter*Lock`/`Mutex.WaitOne` with no `finally` release and, where the release is a statement of the same block, moves the statements between under a `try` and the release into its `finally`. Every gate the C# side learned on real repositories came along - a mutation in a lambda is deferred, a store under `lock gate &lt;| fun () -&gt;` is guarded, an `IList&lt;_&gt;` may be concurrent, a compound emptiness test is a reconnecting holder, not a lazy - and a dry run over fourteen projects of the corpus proposed no fix and no false note. The property suite now typechecks what it generates: eleven rule families of shapes over a two-file script (a stub declares the test-framework and logger namespaces some rules gate on), four properties over every family at once, a per-shape check that each shape reaches its rule - or, for a shape spelled `!FR0162`, that the rule stays quiet on it, the false-positive guard a real repository taught - and a catalog test that every rule has a shape or a stated reason none can. It found three defects: FR0065's whole-setting comment-out put the comment before the `()` and moved the block's offside column, FR0080 read a string literal inside a block comment as code where the compiler stays in the comment, and the shared test-file marker did not know `[&lt;TestCaseSource&gt;]`. FR0012 no longer withholds the `isNull` hint beside a boolean operator: the named-argument guard took every parent application for a call, `||` included, so `x = null || flag` was never rewritten while `if x = null` was; an operator application is not a call, and the replacement stands bare there (`isNull x || flag`). The Visual Studio extension's tagger constructs `FrTagger` with `new`, as FS0760 asks. Two performance rules and a wider FR0106, all gated on a modern framework (the compilation's String must offer its char and span overloads, so netstandard2.0 and net4x passes stay quiet): FR0106 also rewrites a Substring handed to StringBuilder.Append or TextWriter.Write; FR0166 turns a prefix or suffix cut out to compare with a literal into StartsWith/EndsWith with StringComparison.Ordinal (a slice everywhere, a Substring under a .Length guard); FR0167 walks the string itself where `for` or Array.iter/exists/forall read a ToCharArray copy once. FR0066 no longer reads an interpolation hole filled from a constant as a value; FR0127 reads `123456` as a made-up key. FR0015 takes CR0108's shapes: Match(...).Success and the Matches(...).Count tests become Contains (or the ordinal StartsWith), a literal Split becomes String.Split with the separator array.</PackageReleaseNotes>
    <Authors>Tuomas Hietanen</Authors>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Thorium/fsharp-refactor</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageTags>fsharp;analyzers;refactoring;codefix;dotnet-tool</PackageTags>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Unlicense</PackageLicenseExpression>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="FileWalk.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Workspace.fs" />
    <Compile Include="Program.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Update="FSharp.Core" Version="10.1.201" />
    <!-- FSharp.Analyzers.SDK drags in 6.0.0, which is OLDER than the 8.0.0.0 a
         SQLProvider type provider asks for: its MySqlConnector fails to
         initialise against ours and the whole project reports as broken.
         Pinned forward so the provider's own reference resolves. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="8.0.0" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers\FSharp.Refactor.Analyzers.fsproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
